we wanted to create creatures that we  had never seen before on screen bringing  the magical and sometimes terrifying  creatures to harry potter involved loads  of cgi prosthetics and a shocking amount  of built from scratch functional beasts  say hello to buckbeat  hagrid  exactly what is that we can't be the  only ones who wanted to ride a  hippogriff after seeing harry sore  through the air on the stunning creature  building the hippogriff took a lot of  time and skilled workers as they created  a functioning life-sized buckbeak that  continuously got upgrades the model  included individually cut colored and  glued on feathers it just looked alive  you've just never seen anything like it  the whole animatronic functioned and  could be controlled by radio  buckbeak's adventures didn't stop in  studio though its limits were really  tested when brought out to scotland and  filmed in harsh weather conditions  what we see in the end on screen is a  digitally rendered hippogriff that was  based on the movements and behavior of a  regal golden eagle while the whole  process was extremely challenging it was  very worth it in the end we developed a  motion rig that he would ride which  would actually have the animation  driving it daniel radcliffe really was  facing a giant fire-breathing dragon at  times on set for harry potter and the  goblet of fire a huge and shockingly  real hungarian horntail was made and  placed inside a cage for this particular  image to be captured some of the looks  that you see of peril are genuine the  dragon was equipped with a flame thrower  gun concealed in its mouth so they could  set it off whenever they needed bringing  an amazing image and sound to the scene  the concept artist who initially  designed the dragon extended the  signature horns from its tail up its  entire body creating a unique creature  even more impressive than the one  initially described in the book in in  inventing that he had latched on to  something which is immediately unique  interesting oh my god look at that  creature dementors still give us the  creeps the daunting creature with gaping  mouths ready to suck anyone's soul they  can find while cgi brought the  nightmarish creature to life real  dementor models were created and  experimented with underwater huge  puppets with draping black cloth were  made and with the help of a puppeteer  the team did tests filming the dementors  moving underwater and playing it  backwards achieving their eerie and  unnerving movement we really did pay a  lot of attention to the quality and look  of all of that cloth the troll wound up  being a combination of cgi and a giant  practical figure so the young actors had  as close as they could get to a real  troll to work with one version of the  troll was made as a suit that allowed  for a dancer to get inside and fully  perform ballet as a troll while the  legendary suit didn't end up being used  in the films they still provided a ton  of entertainment for the creature  creation crew dobby the house self will  steal all the scenes he's in dobby and  creature may both have been house elves  but they were obviously two very  different types and in the film both of  them were cgi made luckily they don't  just exist in animation  real models were made of them when  creating dobby the goal was to make a  creature audiences could love and they  certainly succeeded in that the voice  was also a key component in making the  character so endearing dobby is  free  animation only enhanced it all with a  quivering lip and the sweet house elf's  big doe eyes creature of course has a  much less inspirational look creature  and dobby are both elves but you've got  a good version of it  and a bad version of it this gentle  strange and misunderstood creature model  was built and scanned by a laser and  used in the animating of the thestrals  we see in the movie something really  intimidating but mesmeric about it the  cgi did a spot-on job at bringing the  right essence to these characters they  were intended to look grotesque but  wound up bringing a very unique beauty  to the film we're making a kids movie  here  we're not making an indictment of human  cruelty  the living dead brought such deep  sadness and woe to the films but that's  not where they started in the early  stages of design the inferior looked  more like rotting corpses and it was a  bit too gruesome overall ultimately they  settled on more realistic human-like  creatures creatures are the  wild side of the magical world as for  the way they moved dancers and movement  artists were brought into the studio and  directed to move around in all sorts of  different ways  this incredible body language helped  bring out the spirit of the ian fairy  the pixies can be devilishly tricky  little blighters  chris columbus took inspiration from a  prior film of his to create these pixies  their look was based on gremlins while  the students of hogwarts didn't really  get the chance to see these creatures  since they were added with cgi in the  editing phase they were directed in  terms of where the pixies would fly what  they'd be doing and how the students  should react to all the chaos  [Music]  for daniel radcliffe the grindylows  looked like two stuntmen in blue suits  underwater and just sort of pulled me to  in front  these half octopus half humans were  designed and digitally rendered so they  could be added to the underwater  struggle and managed to be extremely  effective  the centaurs seem to  portray the best and worst aspects of  human nature centaurs in the harry  potter world came in many forms  from models to costumed actors to cgi  the road to bringing their final look to  the screen was a long one  they are impossible creatures  actors wearing full centaur gear  including extremely detailed makeup and  prosthetics were filmed to give the base  of the image we would see in the final  cut the models were also used by cgi  artists who added the final touches  digitally to create an incredibly  realistic looking creature a major  reason the mandrake creatures look so  creepy is because of just how realistic  they look this has got to be grounded in  reality shockingly no cgi was used these  puppets wrinkly as they are and covered  with dirt managed to look completely  realistic as they moved the animatronics  were so good that working with them was  extremely easy for the young actors  bringing the werewolf to life was a very  long process  that was  i think the worst nightmare i've ever  had the movie used cgi and prosthetics  in the end but a number of methods were  tried beforehand at one point a full  werewolf suit was built but it was made  to be so tiny and skeletal that even the  dancers brought in to wear it couldn't  move around well due to how restrictive  it was stilts were also part of the plan  in the process but unfortunately the  task proved to be impossible to pull off  realistically instead moulds of the  creature were built to scan for cgi and  the lupine actor had special prosthetics  built for his face and the sneak peak of  his back  that turned out not to be practical much  to my relief because it looked really  very uncomfortable to do that over the  years the looks of goblins have changed  so the first time we see them their  characteristics look pretty crude while  these goblins are very stylized they do  feel real because of just how detailed  the masks were 60 different masks wound  up being sculpted  the way they moved was incredibly  natural and made them feel real but it  didn't come easy the goblins had about  three hours worth of makeup prep  resulting in some very frustrated  performers one goblin literally told  chris columbus that he hated him if  you're doing grey back like these are  all greyback maquettes i mean most of  our characters they start off at  sketches greyback provided the team with  an interesting idea to work with the  character is stuck between werewolf and  humans so prosthetics ended up being  used to create a wolfish look for him  peter pettigrew never fails to creep us  out and much of that is thanks to the  impressive and painstakingly built  prosthetics the facial prosthetics built  for the actor included hair on the nose  and around the ears the creepy rat teeth  added to the whole image making him a  truly disturbing creature to finish off  the look and do the animagus to full rat  transformation cgi came through  knowing many of these creatures were  actually built so the actors could  interact with them makes us wish we  could have been on those sets although  we wouldn't have been too crazy about  facing the larger-than-life aragog and  basilisk either way there was a ton of  real-life magic

